// TC: O(n + klogk)
// building a freq map takes O(n) 
// inserting the unique chracters into the priority queue takes O(klogk) k: is no. of unique chracters 
// rebuilding the string takes O(n)
// klogk is effectively constant 

// SC: O(k) for storing the frequencies in the hash map and the heap.

// 3.Optimal Approach (Bucket Sort)
// Becoz the maximum frequency a character can have is the length of the string itself(if the string is entirely one chracter like "aaaa") we can use bucket sort 
// We create an array of buckets where the index of the bucket represents the frequency. We place each character into the bucket corresponding to its count, and then iterate through the buckets backwards to build the final string.

class Solution {
public:
    string frequencySort(string s) {
        //1. Count character frequencies 
        int freq[128] = {0};
        int maxFreq = 0;
        for (char c : s) {
            freq[c]++;
            maxFreq = max(maxFreq, freq[c]);
        }

        //2.Create buckets. The index represents the frequency 
        //Size is maxFreq + 1 coz freq goes from 1 to maxFreq
        vector<vector<char>> buckets(maxFreq + 1);
        for(int i = 0; i < 128; i++) {
            if (freq[i] > 0) {
                buckets[freq[i]].push_back((char)i);
            }
        }

        //3. Rebuild the string by reading buckets from highest freq to lowest 
        string result = "";
        for (int i = maxFreq; i > 0; i--) {
            for (char c : buckets[i]) {
                result.append(i, c);
            }
        }

        return result;
    }
};

TC: O(n) 
we do a single pass to count freq a pass over the 128 const ASCII characters to populate buckets, and final pass proportional to n to construct the string. There is no sorting overhead.

SC: O(n) 
in worst case the buckets vector requires space proportional to the max freq which can be up to N.
